@startuml Parallel Pipeline Performance Model Diagram

title Parallel Pipeline Performance Architecture Model

' Performance objectives and requirements
rectangle "Performance Objectives" as Objectives #LightBlue

' Core performance components
rectangle "Concurrency Control" as Concurrency #LightGreen
rectangle "Resource Management" as Resources #LightYellow
rectangle "Performance Monitoring" as Monitoring #LightCyan
rectangle "Optimization Strategies" as Optimization #LightPink

' Performance relationships
Objectives --> Concurrency : drives
Objectives --> Resources : constrains
Objectives --> Monitoring : validates
Objectives --> Optimization : enables

' Key performance metrics
note right of Objectives
**Performance KPIs:**
- Tasks/sec (throughput)
- ms/task (latency)
- Active tasks (concurrency)
- MB used (memory)
- % CPU (utilization)
end note

note right of Concurrency
**Concurrency Controls:**
- Thread pool sizing
- Task queue management
- Resource throttling
- Deadlock prevention
end note

note right of Resources
**Resource Management:**
- Memory allocation
- Buffer pooling
- Cache hierarchies
- I/O scheduling
end note

note right of Monitoring
**Observability:**
- Real-time metrics
- Performance profiling
- Health monitoring
- Alert management
end note

note as OptimizationPatterns
**Performance Optimization Patterns:**

**Compute Optimization:**
- SIMD vectorization for data processing
- GPU acceleration for compute-intensive tasks
- Algorithm optimization (O(n) improvements)
- Cache-conscious data structures

**Memory Optimization:**
- Memory pool allocation to reduce fragmentation
- Object pooling to eliminate allocation overhead
- Cache-aligned data structures
- NUMA-aware memory placement

**I/O Optimization:**
- Asynchronous I/O for non-blocking operations
- I/O batching to reduce system calls
- Memory-mapped files for large datasets
- Network optimization (TCP tuning, connection pooling)

**Concurrency Optimization:**
- Lock-free data structures where possible
- Fine-grained locking to reduce contention
- Work-stealing schedulers for load balancing
- Thread pool sizing based on workload
end note

@enduml
